}}'''Ohijinja Shrine'' is located in {{ill|lt=Chiba Prefecture|Q80011|WD=Q80011|1=Chiba Prefecture}}{{ill|lt=Funabashi City|Q271417|WD=Q271417|1=Funabashi}}. {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Shikinaisha|1=Shikinaisha|WD=Q134917286}}, and the former {{ill|lt=shakaku|Q10444029|WD=Q10444029|1=Shakaku}} is {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=kensha|1=Ken-sha|WD=Q90139226}}. Its common name is ``Funabashi Daijingu'' (Funabashi Daijingu). In the Engishiki ceremony, it appears under the name ``Katsugo-gun Ifuhi Shrine.''

=== Enshrined God ===
* '''{{ill|lt=Amaterasu Omikami|Q455602|WD=Q455602|lt=Amaterasu Omikami|1=Amaterasu}}'''

===Origin of company name===
There are various theories about the origin of the company's name, ``Ohhi, {{ill|lt=Historical kana usage|UNKNOWN|lt=Old kana usage}}: Ohohi. The main ones are shown below.
* The theory that he is a food god, meaning a great fire or a great meal.
* The theory is that it is a sunset because it is located on a hill that is directly facing the {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=sunset|1=UNKNOWN}} sun.
* Theory that the {{ill|lt=guardian deity|Q767655|WD=Q767655|1=Ujigami}} of the {{ill|lt=Ta clan|Q11430816|WD=Q11430816|lt=Yitomi|1=Ō clan}}, an ancient and powerful ruling family.
* This theory, which means Dainichi, is Otendo-sama (sun god), which {{ill|lt=farmers|Q838811|WD=Q838811|1=Peasant}} in this region had originally worshiped.{{efn|This theory was announced as a theory that added criticism and research to the previous theories, and attracted attention. As if to support this theory, {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Tendo Nenbutsu|1=UNKNOWN}} has been popular in the city since ancient times, and Daijingu Shrine also has a hanging Buddha statue representing the ``Dainichi Nyorai statue'' that was used before the {{ill|lt=Separation of Shinto and Buddhism|Shinto Buddhism Order|WD=Shinto Buddhism Order|ltq=Q1970535}} order was issued. }}

=== History ===
According to the shrine's legend, it began in the year 40 of {{ill|lt=Emperor Keiko|Q329723|WD=Q329723|1=Emperor Keikō}}, when {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Nihon Takeru|1=UNKNOWN}} prayed for the pacification of the eastern region during his expedition to the east. At that time, the local residents were suffering from drought, and when Nihon Takeru prayed for rain at the same time, it was said that it started raining. Originally, the local sun god ``Itobijin (Dainichijin)'' was enshrined at the company, and it attracted the faith of the {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=fishermen|1=UNKNOWN}} in the surrounding area ({{ill|lt=Tokyo Bay|Q141017|WD=Q141017|1=Tokyo Bay}}). The first time I read {{ill|lt=Rokukokushi|Q1151168|WD=Q1151168|lt=Kokushi|1=Rikkokushi}} is in "{{ill|lt=Jihon Sandai Joroku|Q1052981|WD=Q1052981|1=Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku}}" (Japan)|Q1075635|Jōkan}}5 years ({{ill|lt=863 years|Q25052|WD=Q25052|1=863}}) In the article dated May 26, {{ill|lt=Engishiki Shinmyocho|Q11064932|WD=Q11064932|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō}}, it is listed as a small shrine.Later, this area was donated to {{ill|lt=Ise Jingu|Q687168|WD=Q687168|1=Ise Grand Shrine}} and {{ill|lt=Mikuriya|Q11488831|WD=Q11488831|1=mikuriya}} ({{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Natsumi Mikuriya|1=UNKNOWN}}), and Shinmeisha Shrine, which enshrines Amaterasu Omikami, the deity of Ise Grand Shrine, as its guardian, is now {{jalink|Hie Shrine} in {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Natsumidai|1=UNKNOWN}}. (Natsumidai, Funabashi City) ||Hie Shrine}} However, with the decline of Mikuriya, {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Shinmeisha|1=UNKNOWN}} also became obsolete and was enshrined at Ifuhi Shrine. After that, faith in {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Amaterasu no Omikami|1=UNKNOWN}} became stronger, and the name of Ifuhi Shrine was gradually forgotten, and it came to be called exclusively Funabashi Shinmei or Funabashi Daijingu. Until the Sengoku period, the powerful {{ill|lt=Chiba clan|Q2975237|WD=Q2975237|1=Chiba clan}} family {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Tomi clan|1=UNKNOWN}}, who maintained power in this area, served as {{ill|lt=chief priest|Q11453500|WD=Q11453500|1=Gūji}}. Even now, he has changed his surname to Chiba, and members of the same family serve as {{ill|lt=chief priest|Q11453500|WD=Q11453500|1=Gūji}}.

The shrine held many medieval documents, including the {{jalink|Ichikawa-Funabashi War|Q812} from the Boshin War that occurred during the {{ill|lt=Meiji Restoration|Q8707|WD=Q8707|1=Meiji Restoration}}. 56|Battle of Funabashi}} It was burnt down along with the shrine building, and only a few items remain today, including letters from Mitsutane Chiba and a document dating back many years called ``Funabashi Mikuriya Rokuga Goda Kazunoji.'' The main hall was rebuilt in 1896, and the worship hall was rebuilt in 1891. In 1872, it was elevated to the status of a prefectural shrine, and the name was restored to Ifuhi Shrine as recorded in the Engishiki Shinmyocho.It was revered by the imperial court and the shogunate, and members such as {{ill|lt=Taira no Masakado|Q1207652|WD=Q1207652|1=Taira no Masakado}}, {{ill|lt=Minamoto no Yoritomo|Q242800|WD=Q242800|1=Minamoto no Yoritomo}}, and {{ill|lt=Ieyasu Tokugawa|Q171977|WD=Q171977|1=Tokugawa Ieyasu}} donated shrine territories and built and renovated the shrine buildings. At Tokiwa Shrine in the grounds of the shrine, a wooden Ieyasu statue containing Ieyasu's tooth is enshrined. In modern times, successive emperors such as {{ill|lt=Emperor Meiji|Q174231|WD=Q174231|1=Emperor Meiji}}, {{ill|lt=Emperor Taisho|Q157052|WD=Q157052|1=Emperor Taishō}}, and {{ill|lt=Emperor Showa|Q34479|WD=Q34479|1=Hirohito}} have visited the shrine.

=== Rank salary ===
* {{ill|lt=863 years|Q25052|WD=Q25052|1=863}} (Jōkan 5th year) {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Junior Fifth Rank Lower|1=UNKNOWN}} confers {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Seigoi Lower|1=UNKNOWN}}
* {{ill|lt=871|Q24754|WD=Q24754|1=871}} (13th year of Jogan) {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Senior Fifth Rank Upper|1=UNKNOWN}} awarded
* {{ill|lt=874|Q23843|WD=Q23843|1=874}} (16th year of Jogan) Awarded {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Junior Fourth Rank Lower|1=UNKNOWN}} {{efn|{{ill|lt=Nara period|Q189178|WD=Q189178|1=Nara period}}, systems regarding {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Jingi|1=UNKNOWN}} and {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=rituals|1=UNKNOWN}} were put in place, and he began to receive generous protection from the Ritsuryo state. Also, from this time the custom (shakaku) of conferring official rank on gods (shrines) arose. According to records, Honsha was awarded the rank of 4th rank in the 16th year of the Jogan era. This effectively means that he was ranked higher than the fifth rank, which was appointed as {{ill|lt=Shimosa Province|Q1053695|WD=Q1053695|1=Shimōsa Province}}{{ill|lt=Kokushi|Q1351234|WD=Q1351234|1=Kokushi (official)}}, and shows that the head office was positioned as an important shrine at that time. However, there is no clear reason why he was given such a high rank. However, it is generally believed that they were trying to increase the influence of {{ill|lt=Yamato Dynasty|Q2658252|WD=Q2658252|lt=Yamato Dynasty|1=Yamato Kingship}} by bestowing a high rank on the headquarters, which was highly worshiped by the residents of this region. }}

