=== Event ===

=== Dedicated Sumo at Funabashi Daijingu Shrine (October 20th) ===
The votive {{ill|lt=sumo|Q40561|WD=Q40561|1=Sumo}} held as an annual autumn festival has a long history, originating from {{ill|lt=Ieyasu Tokugawa|Q171977|WD=Q171977|1=Tokugawa Ieyasu}}'s Kanran sumo held in {{ill|lt=1590|Q6764|WD=Q6764|1=1590}} ({{ill|lt=Tensho|Q1145058|WD=Q1145058|1=Tenshō (Momoyama period)}}18). At that time, Ieyasu occasionally held falconry events in Shimousa, and had an annex set up as a lodging in {{ill|lt=Funabashi City|Q271417|WD=Q271417|lt=Funabashi|1=Funabashi}}. ({{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Funabashi Goten|1=UNKNOWN}} was located in what is now {{ill|lt=Funabashi City|Q271417|WD=Q271417|1=Funabashi}} Honmachi 4-chome, and was called "Gotenji." The former site is {{ill|lt=Funabashi Toshogu|Q11614238|WD=Q11614238|1=Funabashi-tōshōgū}}) One day, Ieyasu saw a child playing sumo. It is said that the origin of sumo is said to have been dedicated to Funabashi Daijingu Shrine, where people were deeply religious, and later on, {{ill|lt=Kanjin|Q6363447|WD=Q6363447|1=Kanjin}} himself became the origin of {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=amateur sumo|1=UNKNOWN}}.Amateur sumo in Jingu is known for attracting strong wrestlers, and it still contributes to the training of {{ill|lt=sumo wrestlers|Q2727289|WD=Q2727289|1=Rikishi}} from {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Tokatsu|1=UNKNOWN}}. Also, until recently, it was famous for the gathering of enthusiastic {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=spectators|1=UNKNOWN}}, including {{ill|lt=fishermen|Q331432|WD=Q331432|1=Fisherman}}, and spectators dissatisfied with the decisions would throw things and {{jal It was said that there was a scene where ink|Gyoji|Q907647}} was beaten up and he clashed with {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=young people|1=UNKNOWN}} wearing {{ill|lt=Manju|Q11353895|WD=Q11353895|1=Manju}}, which caused a lot of excitement.

